---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import { SITE } from "../../lib/site";
import { localizePath , pick } from "../../i18n/utils";

const lang = (Astro.currentLocale ?? "en") as "en" | "zh";

const slug = "adding-a-server";

const C = pick({
  en: {
    heading: "Adding a server",
    intro:
      "Every connection in VVTerm starts with a server entry. Here is what each field means, how servers belong to workspaces and environments, and how to edit or delete them.",
    meta: "Documentation",
    title: "Adding a server in VVTerm — VVTerm",
    description:
      "Learn the server fields in VVTerm — name, host, port, username, and environment — how servers belong to workspaces, and how to edit or delete them.",
    schemaDescription:
      "How to add a server in VVTerm: name, host, port, username, and environment, plus how servers belong to workspaces and how to edit or delete them.",
    bodyHtml: `
  <p>
    A server in VVTerm is the saved definition of a machine you connect to: where it lives, who you
    log in as, and how you authenticate. Once saved, it appears in your server list and syncs across
    your devices through iCloud. Credentials themselves stay in the Apple Keychain and are never part
    of that synced definition.
  </p>

  <h2>Server fields</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Name</strong> — a label you choose so the server is easy to recognize. It has no effect on the connection.</li>
    <li><strong>Host</strong> — the hostname or IP address to connect to, for example <code>example.com</code> or <code>192.168.1.20</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Port</strong> — the SSH port. This defaults to <code>22</code>; change it only if your server listens on a different port.</li>
    <li><strong>Username</strong> — the account you log in as, such as <code>root</code>, <code>ubuntu</code>, or <code>admin</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Environment</strong> — an optional grouping, like Production or Staging, used to organize servers inside a workspace.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Authentication is set per server. You can use a password, an SSH key, or an SSH key protected by a
    passphrase. The details and where each credential is stored are covered in <a href="/docs/authentication-and-keys">Authentication and SSH keys</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Workspaces and environments</h2>
  <p>
    Every server belongs to a <strong>workspace</strong>. A workspace is a top-level group — you might
    keep one workspace per client, per project, or per team. Inside a workspace, an <strong>environment</strong> further sorts servers so you can keep production and staging hosts
    visually separate and filter the list down to what you need.
  </p>
  <p>
    On the free tier you get one workspace and one server. Pro removes those limits so you can build
    out as many workspaces, environments, and servers as you like. See <a href="/docs/workspaces-and-tabs">Workspaces, environments, and tabs</a> for how this fits
    together with multiple open connections.
  </p>

  <h2>Editing a server</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Open the server's details from your list.</li>
    <li>Change any field — name, host, port, username, environment, or the authentication method.</li>
    <li>Save. The updated definition syncs to your other devices through iCloud.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Changing the host, port, or username affects only future connections. If a session is already
    open, it keeps its original settings until you reconnect.
  </p>

  <h2>Deleting a server</h2>
  <p>
    Deleting a server removes its definition from your list and, after sync, from your other devices.
    Any keychain credential tied to the server is removed with it. If you only want to stop seeing a
    server temporarily, consider moving it to a different environment instead of deleting it, so you
    keep its settings.
  </p>

  <h2>Next steps</h2>
  <p>
    With a server saved, set up how you sign in over at <a href="/docs/authentication-and-keys">Authentication and SSH keys</a>, or learn how your saved
    servers travel between devices in <a href="/docs/icloud-sync">iCloud sync and Keychain</a>.
  </p>
`,
  },
  zh: {
    heading: "添加服务器",
    intro:
      "VVTerm 中的每一次连接都从一条服务器记录开始。这里说明每个字段的含义、服务器如何归属于工作区和环境，以及如何编辑或删除它们。",
    meta: "文档",
    title: "在 VVTerm 中添加服务器 — VVTerm",
    description:
      "了解 VVTerm 中的服务器字段 —— 名称、主机、端口、用户名和环境 —— 服务器如何归属工作区，以及如何编辑或删除它们。",
    schemaDescription:
      "如何在 VVTerm 中添加服务器：名称、主机、端口、用户名和环境，以及服务器如何归属工作区、如何编辑或删除。",
    bodyHtml: `
  <p>
    VVTerm 中的服务器是你所连接机器的已保存定义：它在哪里、你以谁的身份登录，以及你如何认证。保存后，它会出现在你的服务器列表中，并通过 iCloud 在你的设备间同步。密钥本身保存在 Apple Keychain 中，永远不属于那份同步的定义。
  </p>

  <h2>服务器字段</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>名称</strong> —— 你选择的标签，便于识别服务器。它对连接没有影响。</li>
    <li><strong>主机</strong> —— 要连接的主机名或 IP 地址，例如 <code>example.com</code> 或 <code>192.168.1.20</code>。</li>
    <li><strong>端口</strong> —— SSH 端口。默认是 <code>22</code>；只有当你的服务器监听在不同端口时才需更改。</li>
    <li><strong>用户名</strong> —— 你登录所用的账户，例如 <code>root</code>、<code>ubuntu</code> 或 <code>admin</code>。</li>
    <li><strong>环境</strong> —— 可选的分组，如 Production 或 Staging，用于在工作区内整理服务器。</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    认证是按服务器设置的。你可以使用密码、SSH 密钥，或受口令保护的 SSH 密钥。具体细节及每种密钥的存储位置见<a href="/zh/docs/authentication-and-keys">认证与 SSH 密钥</a>。
  </p>

  <h2>工作区与环境</h2>
  <p>
    每台服务器都归属于一个<strong>工作区</strong>。工作区是顶层分组 —— 你可以为每个客户、每个项目或每个团队保留一个工作区。在工作区内，<strong>环境</strong>进一步对服务器排序，让你把生产和预发主机在视觉上分开，并把列表筛选到你需要的范围。
  </p>
  <p>
    免费版提供一个工作区和一台服务器。Pro 取消了这些限制，让你能构建任意多的工作区、环境和服务器。关于这如何与多个打开的连接配合，参见<a href="/zh/docs/workspaces-and-tabs">工作区、环境与标签页</a>。
  </p>

  <h2>编辑服务器</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>从列表中打开服务器的详情。</li>
    <li>更改任意字段 —— 名称、主机、端口、用户名、环境或认证方式。</li>
    <li>保存。更新后的定义会通过 iCloud 同步到你的其他设备。</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    更改主机、端口或用户名只影响之后的连接。如果某个会话已经打开，它会保留原始设置，直到你重新连接。
  </p>

  <h2>删除服务器</h2>
  <p>
    删除服务器会把它的定义从列表中移除，并在同步后从你的其他设备移除。与该服务器绑定的任何钥匙串密钥也会随之删除。如果你只想暂时不看到某台服务器，可以考虑把它移到另一个环境而非删除，这样可以保留它的设置。
  </p>

  <h2>后续步骤</h2>
  <p>
    保存好服务器后，前往<a href="/zh/docs/authentication-and-keys">认证与 SSH 密钥</a>设置你的登录方式，或在<a href="/zh/docs/icloud-sync">iCloud 同步与 Keychain</a>中了解你保存的服务器如何在设备间流转。
  </p>
`,
  },
}, lang);

const articleSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "TechArticle",
  name: C.heading,
  description: C.schemaDescription,
  url: `${SITE.siteUrl}${localizePath(`/docs/${slug}`, lang)}`,
};
---

<DocsLayout
  kind="docs"
  activeSlug={slug}
  heading={C.heading}
  intro={C.intro}
  meta={C.meta}
  title={C.title}
  description={C.description}
  canonicalPath={`/docs/${slug}`}
  structuredData={[articleSchema]}
>
  <Fragment set:html={C.bodyHtml} />
</DocsLayout>
